  "thm-cg-bruhat-parabolic-projection-and-quotients": {
   "citations": [
    {
     "fact": "F1",
     "source": "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
     "source_section": "Definition",
     "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix, let $W$ be the presented group with\nlength function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), and\nlet $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$ be the set of reflections of $W$\n([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]). Throughout, $I$ and $J$ denote\nsubsets of $S$.\n\n**(1) Standard parabolic subgroups.** For $I\\subseteq S$ put\n\n$$W_I:=\\langle s:s\\in I\\rangle\\le W,$$\n\nthe subgroup generated by $I$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]]); $W_I$ is the\n**standard parabolic subgroup** of $(W,S)$ of type $I$. Thus\n$W_\\emptyset=\\{1\\}$ and $W_S=W$. By\n[[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1),(2): for\nevery $w\\in W$ one has $w\\in W_I$ if and only if $S(w)\\subseteq I$, where\n$S(w)$ is the (well-defined) set of letters of any reduced expression of $w$;\n$(W_I,I)$ is a Coxeter system; its intrinsic length function agrees with\n$\\ell|_{W_I}$; and $W_I\\cap S=I$.\n\n**(2) Descents and the one-sided quotients.** For $w\\in W$ put\n\n$$D_L(w):=\\{s\\in S:\\ell(sw)<\\ell(w)\\},\\qquad D_R(w):=\\{s\\in S:\\ell(ws)<\\ell(w)\\};$$\n\neach of $\\ell(sw)-\\ell(w)$ and $\\ell(ws)-\\ell(w)$ lies in $\\{\\pm1\\}$\n([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (1)). Define\n\n$$W^I:=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\}=\\{w\\in W:D_R(w)\\cap I=\\emptyset\\},$$\n\n$${}^IW:=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\}=\\{w\\in W:D_L(w)\\cap I=\\emptyset\\}.$$\n\nConventions are fixed here: in ${}^IW$ the subset $I$ is tested on the left, in\n$W^I$ it is tested on the right; $\\ell(sw)$ means left multiplication by $s$\nand $\\ell(ws)$ right multiplication. The interpretation of these sets as sets\nof coset representatives is not part of the definition; it is quoted from\n[[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) and\nre-examined in [[thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization]] (3):\nevery $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization\n\n$$w=u\\,d\\qquad(u\\in W_I,\\ d\\in{}^IW),\\qquad \\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d),$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the unique element of minimal length in the left coset $W_Iw$, and\nit satisfies $\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$ for all $u\\in W_I$; symmetrically every\n$w\\in W$ has a unique factorization\n\n$$w=d\\,v\\qquad(d\\in W^I,\\ v\\in W_I),\\qquad \\ell(w)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v),$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the unique element of minimal length in the right coset $wW_I$, and\nit satisfies $\\ell(dv)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)$ for all $v\\in W_I$. Thus ${}^IW$\nconsists exactly of the elements of minimal length in the left cosets\n$W_Iw=\\{uw:u\\in W_I\\}$ and $W^I$ consists exactly of the elements of minimal\nlength in the sets $wW_I=\\{wv:v\\in W_I\\}$. Inversion gives\n$W^I=({}^IW)^{-1}$, because $\\ell(x)=\\ell(x^{-1})$ and $(sw)^{-1}=w^{-1}s$.\n\n**(3) The two-sided quotients.** Define\n\n$${}^IW^J:={}^IW\\cap W^J=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\ \\text{ and }\\ \\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in J\\};$$\n\nthus ${}^\\emptyset W^J=W^J$, ${}^IW^\\emptyset={}^IW$ and ${}^\\emptyset\nW^\\emptyset=W$, and the members of ${}^IW^J$ are exactly the elements of $W$\nwith no left descent in $I$ and no right descent in $J$. Whether ${}^IW^J$ is a\nset of representatives of the double cosets $W_IwW_J$ is again a theorem, not a\ndefinition; it is proved in\n[[thm-cg-double-coset-unique-minimum-and-normal-form]].\n\n**(4) Parabolic subgroups and reflection subgroups.** A subgroup $H\\le W$ is a\n**parabolic subgroup** of $(W,S)$ if\n\n$$H=wW_Iw^{-1}\\qquad\\text{for some }w\\in W,\\ I\\subseteq S;$$\n\na parabolic subgroup with $w=1$ is *standard*. A subgroup $H\\le W$ is a\n**reflection subgroup** if $H=\\langle H\\cap T\\rangle$, i.e. if $H$ is generated\nby the reflections it contains. Every parabolic subgroup is a reflection\nsubgroup, since $wW_Iw^{-1}=\\langle wsw^{-1}:s\\in I\\rangle$ and\n$wsw^{-1}\\in T$ for all $s\\in S$, $w\\in W$. No converse is asserted: it is not\nclaimed here that a subgroup generated by reflections is parabolic, and the\ncompanion examples page computes test cases for the two distinctions created by\nthis definition --- standard parabolic versus parabolic, and reflection subgroup\nversus parabolic subgroup.",
     "uses": [
      "1.1",
      "1.2",
      "2.1",
      "3.1",
      "3.2",
